1
00:00:00,690 --> 00:00:04,440
When people decide to move to a new
domain, most of them do the same thing.

2
00:00:04,740 --> 00:00:08,800
They buy the new one, switch on
"forwarding" at the registrar, type the

3
00:00:08,850 --> 00:00:11,010
old address, and land on the new site.

4
00:00:11,130 --> 00:00:12,130
And honestly?

5
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:12,940
Fair enough.

6
00:00:12,990 --> 00:00:14,230
It looks done.

7
00:00:16,005 --> 00:00:18,705
But here's what a half-done move does, a
few weeks later.

8
00:00:18,885 --> 00:00:21,945
Some old links open with a warning: "Not
secure."

9
00:00:22,305 --> 00:00:25,675
Others dump people on the homepage, not
the page they wanted.

10
00:00:25,725 --> 00:00:26,985
Some pages are just…

11
00:00:27,075 --> 00:00:27,865
gone.

12
00:00:27,915 --> 00:00:30,525
Google can't follow the move, so your
rankings slip.

13
00:00:30,825 --> 00:00:32,445
And the QR code on your menu?

14
00:00:32,655 --> 00:00:34,395
The link in that email from last year?

15
00:00:34,755 --> 00:00:35,755
Dead.

16
00:00:35,957 --> 00:00:38,927
So how do you move your domain the right
way, and keep it secure?

17
00:00:39,287 --> 00:00:41,037
It's simpler than you think.

18
00:00:43,627 --> 00:00:45,847
Three ideas, and then we do it.

19
00:00:47,211 --> 00:00:49,971
Idea one: a redirect is a note on the
door.

20
00:00:50,421 --> 00:00:52,971
Your old domain is a house you've moved
out of.

21
00:00:53,241 --> 00:00:54,501
People still show up.

22
00:00:54,861 --> 00:00:57,741
The note says: we moved, here's the new
address.

23
00:00:57,981 --> 00:01:01,401
A good note sends them to the right room,
not just the front door.

24
00:01:01,731 --> 00:01:05,971
The internet has a specific note for this:
a 301.

25
00:01:06,021 --> 00:01:08,161
It means "moved permanently."

26
00:01:08,211 --> 00:01:12,871
Browsers follow it instantly, and Google
reads it as: update your records, this is

27
00:01:12,921 --> 00:01:13,921
the new home.

28
00:01:14,257 --> 00:01:17,707
Idea two: DNS is the internet's address
book.

29
00:01:17,977 --> 00:01:22,247
Type a domain, and your browser looks it
up in that book to find a number: the

30
00:01:22,297 --> 00:01:23,437
server to talk to.

31
00:01:23,737 --> 00:01:25,507
Those entries are called records.

32
00:01:25,777 --> 00:01:30,077
Moving the right way means changing a
couple of those records, so the old domain

33
00:01:30,127 --> 00:01:32,227
points at something that hands out the
note.

34
00:01:32,842 --> 00:01:35,632
Idea three: the padlock is an ID card.

35
00:01:36,082 --> 00:01:40,432
Over HTTPS, a site shows an ID card first.

36
00:01:40,642 --> 00:01:42,112
It's called a certificate.

37
00:01:42,382 --> 00:01:46,342
It proves the site is who it says it is,
and it keeps the connection private.

38
00:01:46,612 --> 00:01:48,622
No certificate, no padlock.

39
00:01:48,922 --> 00:01:50,752
A warning instead of your page.

40
00:01:51,022 --> 00:01:52,022
Here's the catch.

41
00:01:52,192 --> 00:01:56,672
Your old domain needs its own ID card,
even if all it does is point somewhere

42
00:01:56,722 --> 00:01:57,542
else.

43
00:01:57,592 --> 00:02:00,442
The browser checks the ID before it reads
the note.

44
00:02:01,093 --> 00:02:05,273
And browsers are getting stricter: Chrome
is rolling out a warning before it opens

45
00:02:05,323 --> 00:02:07,093
public sites that aren't secure.

46
00:02:07,483 --> 00:02:11,623
To be fair to registrars: some forwarding
handles the ID card for you.

47
00:02:11,803 --> 00:02:14,353
Some doesn't, and the certificate is on
you.

48
00:02:14,533 --> 00:02:16,453
So, some do, some don't.

49
00:02:16,753 --> 00:02:21,223
Here's how to check: type your old address
with https in front.

50
00:02:21,613 --> 00:02:22,253
Padlock?

51
00:02:22,303 --> 00:02:23,033
You're fine.

52
00:02:23,083 --> 00:02:23,783
Warning?

53
00:02:23,833 --> 00:02:24,653
Keep watching.

54
00:02:24,703 --> 00:02:25,463
That's it.

55
00:02:25,513 --> 00:02:28,993
Three ideas: the note, the address book,
the ID card.

56
00:02:29,233 --> 00:02:30,713
Now let's use them.

57
00:02:31,946 --> 00:02:33,446
Here's how to do it the right way.

58
00:02:33,656 --> 00:02:37,136
Full disclosure: we make RedirHub, so
that's what I'll use.

59
00:02:37,406 --> 00:02:41,976
It handles the note and the ID card for
you, but the steps are the same idea

60
00:02:42,026 --> 00:02:43,026
anywhere.

61
00:02:43,196 --> 00:02:45,566
Old domain: mydomain.com.

62
00:02:45,896 --> 00:02:48,236
New site: mybrand.com.

63
00:02:48,860 --> 00:02:50,720
Step one: write the note.

64
00:02:50,960 --> 00:02:53,480
Links → Create → Domain Redirect.

65
00:02:53,690 --> 00:02:56,090
Redirect from: mydomain.com.

66
00:02:56,360 --> 00:03:02,300
Redirect to: https://mybrand.com.

67
00:03:02,750 --> 00:03:04,470
Type: 301.

68
00:03:04,520 --> 00:03:05,720
And keep the path on.

69
00:03:05,960 --> 00:03:08,090
That's the right-room-not-the-front-door
part.

70
00:03:08,270 --> 00:03:09,120
Save.

71
00:03:09,170 --> 00:03:10,310
One idea down.

72
00:03:10,972 --> 00:03:12,872
Step two: the address book.

73
00:03:12,922 --> 00:03:16,252
RedirHub shows you exactly which records
to change.

74
00:03:16,492 --> 00:03:22,112
For a root domain, that's two: an A
record, the number to point at, and a TXT

75
00:03:22,162 --> 00:03:24,832
record, which proves the domain is yours.

76
00:03:25,102 --> 00:03:29,452
Plus a CNAME for the www version.

77
00:03:29,722 --> 00:03:30,662
Quick tip.

78
00:03:30,712 --> 00:03:33,982
Before you touch anything in your DNS,
screenshot it.

79
00:03:34,102 --> 00:03:36,532
Thirty seconds now, zero regret later.

80
00:03:37,264 --> 00:03:41,554
Now open your DNS, wherever your domain
lives, and add those records.

81
00:03:41,644 --> 00:03:42,934
Copy and paste.

82
00:03:43,204 --> 00:03:45,034
And copy yours, not mine.

83
00:03:45,334 --> 00:03:48,304
Back in RedirHub, it checks every few
seconds.

84
00:03:48,544 --> 00:03:50,074
The address book updates…

85
00:03:50,224 --> 00:03:52,774
and the ID card is issued automatically.

86
00:03:53,014 --> 00:03:55,214
Two green checks: DNS.

87
00:03:55,264 --> 00:03:56,584
HTTPS.

88
00:03:56,644 --> 00:03:57,914
That's the whole setup.

89
00:03:57,964 --> 00:04:00,104
Idea three took care of itself.

90
00:04:01,317 --> 00:04:02,137
Let's test it.

91
00:04:02,187 --> 00:04:05,037
Open a browser, type mydomain.com…

92
00:04:05,397 --> 00:04:06,807
…and it's still the old site.

93
00:04:06,927 --> 00:04:07,657
Hold on.

94
00:04:07,707 --> 00:04:09,267
Your redirect might be fine.

95
00:04:09,537 --> 00:04:11,257
It's your test that's wrong.

96
00:04:12,943 --> 00:04:16,934
Browsers keep copies of pages they've
seen, so you may be looking at yesterday's

97
00:04:16,984 --> 00:04:17,723
site.

98
00:04:17,773 --> 00:04:20,533
First rule of testing a move: a private
window.

99
00:04:20,773 --> 00:04:22,603
No copies, no leftovers.

100
00:04:22,873 --> 00:04:24,763
Same address, private window…

101
00:04:25,033 --> 00:04:25,943
and there it is.

102
00:04:25,993 --> 00:04:27,413
mybrand.com.

103
00:04:27,463 --> 00:04:28,583
Padlock.

104
00:04:33,121 --> 00:04:34,121
Three quick checks.

105
00:04:35,391 --> 00:04:40,431
One: the old domain, with https in front,
lands on the new site with a padlock.

106
00:04:40,581 --> 00:04:44,601
Two: an old deep link lands on the same
page, not the homepage.

107
00:04:44,871 --> 00:04:47,691
Three: the www version works too.

108
00:04:47,991 --> 00:04:49,321
All three green.

109
00:04:50,616 --> 00:04:52,116
Two things before you go.

110
00:04:52,596 --> 00:04:56,856
A padlock means the connection is private,
not that the site is honest.

111
00:04:57,156 --> 00:05:00,006
It's an ID card, not a character
reference.

112
00:05:00,276 --> 00:05:02,346
And don't let the old domain expire.

113
00:05:02,736 --> 00:05:07,876
If it lapses, someone else can register
it, and every old link and printed QR code

114
00:05:07,926 --> 00:05:08,926
points at them.

115
00:05:09,096 --> 00:05:12,606
Keep renewing it as long as anyone might
still have the old address.

116
00:05:13,302 --> 00:05:14,302
Remember the start?

117
00:05:14,412 --> 00:05:17,262
Forwarding on, old link opens, looks done.

118
00:05:17,352 --> 00:05:18,762
Now it is.

